  "notes": "A ceiling is not in the standard; a bound on what an end-point will echo is a deployment's own protection against a message whose only content is work. What the standard *does* fix is what happens when the bound is reached, and it is not truncation: §6.2 obliges the end-point to return the same submessage for every token the message contains, so answering thirty-three with thirty-two would be a non-conforming reply that looks like a conforming one. Refusing the request says so out loud. The pair to this case is `ecma-430/40`, where thirty-two are all returned. `endpoint.reply` is absent because the refusal happens before the handler is asked anything. Not a rule of the standard; an implementation with no ceiling replies to this request and should skip this case by name, saying so — which is why the case sits here and its pair, `ecma-430/40`, does not."
